            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house is relatively airtight and it does have a high efficiency dual core HRV, so reducing unnecessary air leakage is good to achieve. Please note that although your house has an HRV, I'm not sure how well it is set up or operates as it appears to have had the electrical controls modified. You should have it checked out by a mechanical contractor in Yellowknife who is currently installing new HRV's and services older ones (ask for references).



The following are locations where air leakage can be reduced:



** The chimney will be eliminated when you upgrade to a high efficiency, direct-vent boiler combi system.



** The combustion air pipe can also be eliminated (although the air leakage was partially reduced as someone has tried to plug the pipe).



** The hole in the wall in your master bedroom closed (between the cabinet &amp; the wall (where the wire comes out)



** The current propane fireplace has significant air leakage around it



** The trim along the top of the patio door (where the trim meets the wall) needs to be caulked. Check all the other window &amp; door trim along the top as well.



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 168 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.9 GJ</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

The foundation of your house is a heated crawlspace. I don't think there is a dedicated heating loop and a thermostat in the crawlspace, however it is being heated from the in-floor heating in the floor above the crawlspace. Someone has installed some foil into the floor joist cavity to try &amp; reflect the heat up to the main floor, however there are many areas where the foil has fallen down or is partially open, so the in-floor heat is actually heating both the crawlspace &amp; the upper main floor.



What I most commonly see for in-floor heat above a crawlspace is to have both a reflective heat shield with some insulation installed below the foil. The in-floor heat can then heat the main floor based on the thermostat settings. If the crawlspace in intended to be heated, there would then be another heat loop in the crawlspace with its' own thermostat, usually set to about 10C.



As you have both a water &amp; septic tank in the crawlspace, and the water tank appears to be pushing upward, you may want to have an engineer review "what's going on" down there before you make any changes. Perhaps there is too much heat currently in the crawl that is causing some heaving ?? An expert opinion should be obtained.



Savings: It is tough to estimate the savings of this upgrade as the crawlspace &amp; main floor are currently both heated. Installing insulation between 2 heated floors doesn't reflect much savings in the computer model.</foundation>
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

The domestic hot water system for your house is integrated into the boiler, so please see my upgrade recommendation &amp; energy/cost savings within the previous section about the Heating System.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 81% efficient oil boiler that has 9 separate heat zones with 9 thermostats. In my opinion, this is too many zones and the boiler is a slave to the competing demands of all the various zones and is likely "short cycling" and operating at an efficiency much lower than it was originally designed to provide. The current boiler is also way oversized (at 152,200 BTU of output) and does not modulate to meet the heat demand so it will once again be operating outside of its' peak efficiency rating.



The boiler has an integrated domestic hot water jacket that heats your hot water, with an electric hot water tank for some additional storage. During the summer (when the boiler is off) the hot water for your house is satisfied by using the electric hot water tank in "electric" mode. The efficiency for this older oil boiler providing domestic hot water is about 56%,



A few other comments:

- There is a "heat exchange" manifold in the mechanical room that operates as big radiator

- The boiler requires combustion air, however the combustion air supply pipe has been blocked off

- There is some sort of heat ductwork from the mechanical room that is intended to transfer the excess heat from the boiler room and blow it into the master bedroom closet



I have modeled an upgrade from this older oil boiler &amp; domestic hot water "combo" system to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas "combi boiler" with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. This new boiler will be able to modulate to better meet the demands of the various heat zones in your house while maintaining efficiency. It will also increase the efficiency of the domestic hot water to about 80%.



Another key advantage of upgrading to this new boiler is that the current chimney &amp; combustion air pipe can be eliminated, as the new condensing boilers are direct vent, via PVC pipe !!



Savings: By replacing your older oil boiler with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas boiler, that also provides your domestic hot water at a much higher efficiency, you could potentially save $ 2,733 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 57.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. There is also a rebate for upgrading the circulation pumps to high efficiency ECM pumps - $175 per pump, with up to 2 pumps per fiscal year. See my Additional Comments section of this report for additional information about ECM pumps.



The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has three toilets and two of them are older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorsecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood fan, the master bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 8.0 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil boiler vents up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting. The dedicated combustion air pipe for the boiler was blocked off at the time of my visit.



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - A dual core HRV, that is high efficiency, however it appears to have had the controls modified

        - A kitchen range hood, vent to the outside

        - One bathroom exhaust fan in the master bathroom

        - A clothes dryer, vented to the outside



You should have the HRV checked out by a mechanical contractor in Yellowknife who is currently installing new HRV's and services older ones (ask for references).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $0.68 per litre

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
